A||||4||SLYGUY||Coolant Drip...||||5||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||12-19-2001||03:50 AM||||Hey guys and gals,<P>Kinda a stupid question, but here goes:<P>When I start my car, after a few minutes of it warming up I notice a green pool forming just in front of the driver's side rear wheel. When I poked my head under there I could see a drip from the hose clamp that secures the radiator hose to the metal tube that comes from the radiator. I was able to tighten the clamp a little but the drip continues until the car is warmed up and then stops. I'm sure it has to do with the pressure from the water pump till the thermostat opens. My question is this:<P>What path does that hose travel? Where does it enter the engine/water pump or filler neck? I'm thinking of just replacing both hoses but need some more info.<P>Thanks in advance.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||GTDude||12-19-2001||08:11 AM||||Sounds like you're on the right track there, but you might want to closely check the metal tube for rust holes while you're at it.  Also, be sure it's not a freeze plug leaking and dripping down on the hose.  Good luck.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_3.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-19-2001||11:17 AM||||A couple things... The rubber hose on the left side of the car goes from the stainless tube under the drivers door to a painted steel tube that extends pretty much from the left side of the car to the right, under the front (firewall side) exhaust manifold. You can see the left end of this tube in front of your tranny, about two feet directly below the rubber air intake duct. It's very common for *that* tube to rust and leak. Especially on 88s. <P>Rodney Dickman sells stainless steel replacements. Better quality than the original, and cheaper than GM's price (GM discontinued them, BTW.)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html</A>   <P>Here's a direct link to the part... <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/n9.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/n9.html</A>  <P>It's a *lot* of fun to replace.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Also check the drain in the back of your stainless tube. It's an allen screw. I suppose they can get loose and leak.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||K2m4||12-19-2001||01:50 PM||||Same thing happened with my 2M4, the clips that hold the stainless steel pipe is steel and it causes the pipe to rot under the clip. heres is what i did to fix mine.<BR>I drained the system but i had to do it the hard way. The drain plug was seized. so i jacked up the ass end, disconnected the bube up front. Went to the hard ware store and purchased barco-bond. cleaned up the rust and barco bonded the rust holes. let it set for a day to cure. i cleaned up the clips sandblasted and painted them. before i installe d them back i used gasket maker and made a 1/4 thick rubber gasket to reside between the clip and the pipe to prevent further corrosion...It all worked like a charm..yeah it took a whole day to do...but most of the day you stare at the barco bond waiting for it to cure!<P><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||12-19-2001||02:40 PM||||Thank you so much for all of your help. I had no idea what it might be, now I do. <P>You guys kick as$!!<P>Sly<P><BR>(P.S. I'm sure OEM is much more but the tube at Rodney's is EXPENSIVE!!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
